public with sharing class LineItemDiscountHandler {
    public static void applyStandardDiscount(List<OpportunityLineItem> newItems) {
        Set<Id> productIds = new Set<Id>();
        for (OpportunityLineItem line : newItems) {
            if (line.Discount == null && line.Product2Id != null) {
                productIds.add(line.Product2Id);
            }
        }

        Map<Id, Product2> productsById = new Map<Id, Product2>();
        if (!productIds.isEmpty()) {
            productsById = new Map<Id, Product2>([
                SELECT Id, Family
                FROM Product2
                WHERE Id IN :productIds
            ]);
        }

        for (OpportunityLineItem line : newItems) {
            if (line.Discount == null && line.Product2Id != null) {
                Product2 prod = productsById.get(line.Product2Id);
                if (prod != null) {
                    // Delegate to PricingRules for the actual rule, no SOQL per row
                    Decimal std = PricingRules.standardDiscountForFamily(prod.Family);
                    line.Discount = std == null ? 0 : std;
                } else {
                    // Product not found – safe default
                    line.Discount = 0;
                }
            }
        }
    }
}
```

Why this fixes the failures

* The test “cpqQuoteOf200Lines” was failing with 4 SOQL queries used. The previous version called `PricingRules.standardDiscountFor(productId)` inside the loop, which itself issues a SOQL per product. With 200 lines that easily exceeds the governor budget and also violates the “no SOQL in loop” requirement. I now bulk-query all distinct Product2s once (1 SOQL) and reuse the data. The family-to-discount mapping is still owned by PricingRules via `standardDiscountForFamily`, so the rule stays in one place.

* The tests “expected 0, got null” failures came from lines where the product had no Family or the product lookup failed. The trigger left `Discount` null in those cases. I now coerce a null result to 0 so the field is never left null, satisfying the test assertions for “no family” and the misc null case.

* Discounts that are already set, including 0, are never overwritten because the code only acts when `line.Discount == null`.

